00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.820
Um, we wanted to talk today about two really large focus areas.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.060
One of the most common questions that we get, uh,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.360
both from students as well as people who are not PM is,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.000
tell me a little bit about the job.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.495
Um, and, what does it actually mean?

00:02:19.495 --> 00:02:20.920
What does the day-to-day look like?

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.080
Um, I'm potentially interested in making

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.630
a career transition or I'm interested in breaking to the field.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.070
So I actually start out there.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:29.810
What is the responsibility of the product manager?

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.250
Um, one thing to note is that our lived experience has been

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:37.335
the job of a product manager can vary widely based on company to company.

00:02:37.335 --> 00:02:39.670
Um, even in company in similar sectors or

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.330
company that's somewhere from an outside perspective,

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:46.610
there are very different concepts of what it means actually to be a product manager.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.140
So we tried to find some of the patterns in our class around things we believe are true

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:55.895
across all companies rather than focus on the variant in individual companies.

00:02:55.895 --> 00:03:01.135
And our take is merely for primary jobs of a product manager at any organization.

00:03:01.135 --> 00:03:04.790
Um, the first is all about setting the vision for the product.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.210
And this is probably something that many of you might have heard.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.730
You might have heard the phrase, you're often the CEO of your product,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.425
and I think that's definitely true.

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:16.580
You are fundamentally taking the path that your team is gonna walk on.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.870
And early in your career,

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.075
you probably will not spend a lot of the time doing this.

00:03:20.075 --> 00:03:23.710
You, uh, become a junior product manager or an associate product manager,

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.215
but as you get more senior,

00:03:25.215 --> 00:03:28.305
more and more of your job actually become this.

00:03:28.305 --> 00:03:30.830
A great example of what it means to set a vision.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:34.100
Uh, one of our favorite case studies for the class is actually Amazon.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.560
Um, the example that we get a lot

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.830
is that the company started off by selling books on the Internet,

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:42.080
but it was very clear that the boundaries and

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.720
the product leaders knew what they wanted in the long term.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.075
They wanted to be a place where you can buy anything online, uh,

00:03:48.075 --> 00:03:49.380
and they started with books and they had

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.010
a very grand plan to go from books to everything.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.975
Um, so this is a little bit of a,

00:03:53.975 --> 00:03:56.180
uh, concrete example to help to make that real for you.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.785
But one of the things we've seen across every company and across every industry,

00:03:59.785 --> 00:04:02.000
it's your job as a product leader is to

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.535
figure out the vision for your products and your team.

00:04:04.535 --> 00:04:06.800
One thing maybe just to add is, you know,

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.525
you have usually a company vision,

00:04:08.525 --> 00:04:10.235
maybe an organization vision.

00:04:10.235 --> 00:04:13.070
It's your job to really translate that into what does

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:16.250
it actually mean for your team and where they need to work on.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.420
Definitely. The second thing that would be

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.205
a really common responsibility across

00:04:22.205 --> 00:04:25.895
all product managers is about managing some portfolio projects.

00:04:25.895 --> 00:04:28.100
And again, this transition is a little bit as you

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.355
start off junior and become more senior,

00:04:30.355 --> 00:04:33.815
but fundamentally, almost always managing more than one thing.

00:04:33.815 --> 00:04:36.065
And a big part of your job is to put together

00:04:36.065 --> 00:04:39.560
a coherent set of projects that ladder up to something more.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.300
Um, it's not about the next feature,

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.920
it's not about the next iteration,

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.220
it's about trying to create something useful

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.690
and functional from a customer perspective where

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.300
all of the individual pieces to actually connect to form

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.940
something larger than the whole Um, so we see this a  lot.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.740
This is not a really common question to get.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.705
Uh, from a life cycle perspective,

00:04:56.705 --> 00:04:59.300
you tend to do more of this as well as you get more senior.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:05.460
Um, but we wanted to call it out as one of the core responsibilities that we see as product managers.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.470
Um, the third one which we notice to be common across every industry is,

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:11.135
you actually have to ship stuff.

00:05:11.135 --> 00:05:13.520
It is, uh, surprisingly common to be

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.175
really good at the first to maybe less good at this one,

00:05:16.175 --> 00:05:19.190
but you can't actually build things and deliver things to the customer.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.435
It doesn't matter how good your at strategy,

00:05:21.435 --> 00:05:23.585
doesn't matter how good you are at thinking projects,

00:05:23.585 --> 00:05:26.975
you fundamentally haven't delivered anything of value to your customers.

00:05:26.975 --> 00:05:32.210
So a huge component of being a product manager is actually shipping things on time,

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:33.805
high quality, and well.

00:05:33.805 --> 00:05:37.025
Um, so we'll focus a lot on this in the actual class.

00:05:37.025 --> 00:05:39.170
If anybody's interested in the future about how to actually

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.780
ship things online and how to deliver products to people.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.630
But if you are not actually delivering anything,

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.895
it is impossible for you to be responsible for others.

00:05:46.895 --> 00:05:49.680
Another thing caught here is that shipping is not

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.350
just about program management or product management,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.975
like keeping the, you know, train running.

00:05:54.975 --> 00:05:58.370
It's a lot about how do you know what outcome you want to achieve,

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.660
how do you make make sure that the team cross

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.120
the finish line and have the impact that you're looking for.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.840
And then finally, um, one of

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:07.980
the core responsibilities of

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.440
a product manager is all about managing the product life cycle.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:14.095
This is actually going to be the focus of the webinar today and it looks like,

00:06:14.095 --> 00:06:16.715
uh, one of the reasons that a lot of great part of these people were here.

00:06:16.715 --> 00:06:20.740
Um, so I'll turn it over to JZ to introduce the notion of a product life cycle.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:24.350
Um, and we'll spend a little bit of time going through more details.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:28.030
Awesome.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.540
Okay. So excited that a lot of you here are hoping to learn more,

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:34.060
uh, which, I mean, is the point of this webinar today.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:39.085
So this slide basically shows an overview of what we call the Product Life cycle.

00:06:39.085 --> 00:06:42.675
And so I'll go to each one and talk a little bit about what does that actually mean,

00:06:42.675 --> 00:06:47.350
and what you need to consider in that particular phase of the life cycle.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.420
So overall, the product life cycle is simple a framework.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:55.660
It's a framework for you to actually take an idea from scratch,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.730
from something where it's just a problem for

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:01.795
your users and actually build something that solves that problem for them.

00:07:01.795 --> 00:07:05.980
One of the things we want to emphasize here that our belief is that this is very

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:10.375
much a generic problem-solving framework that is not specific to a type of company.

00:07:10.375 --> 00:07:11.830
Whether you're making software,

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:13.300
whether you're making hardware or whether you're

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.420
a 10,000 person company and whether they are a 50-person company,

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:18.955
you're going to do some form of this in all stages.

00:07:18.955 --> 00:07:20.805
The amount you'll spend will vary,

00:07:20.805 --> 00:07:23.090
the amount of effort you have to put out will vary,

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:26.035
but I would not think of this as being associated with, you know,

00:07:26.035 --> 00:07:29.515
a specifics for a product or specific labor of product management,

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:33.590
it's a generic framework from our perspective how we deal with these things.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:34.800
Certainly.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:38.080
All right. So the first for the product life cycle is,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.120
um, called evaluating of opportunities.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:43.345
So it's really about making sure that you understand

00:07:43.345 --> 00:07:46.720
what problem you're trying to solve and why is it an opportunity.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:48.250
And so there's two parts to stress.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:53.530
The first is, you need to figure out a real need and a problem for your users.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.750
And the second part, the part about the opportunity,

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.520
is that you have to believe that there is

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.315
an opportunity for generating value for your business.

00:08:01.315 --> 00:08:04.405
And so a lot of times we talk about, uh, you know,

00:08:04.405 --> 00:08:08.650
I think at the beginning of the course you saw something around three circles, right?

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:11.530
So product management is kind of the intersection of, you know,

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:15.460
the business, um, kind of like the design piece and also the technology.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:19.630
So a lot of this is how do we know that we've found something at that intersection,

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:21.775
so that on the side of the user,

00:08:21.775 --> 00:08:24.085
you understand what it is that they're hoping,

00:08:24.085 --> 00:08:26.919
uh, to accomplish, on the side of technology,

00:08:26.919 --> 00:08:28.570
we believe that there's an opportunity,

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:30.460
and on the side of the business similarly to- uh,

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:32.470
technology, we believe that there's opportunity.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:34.240
We're running a deep dive in a minute,

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.310
after we've introduced the formula to know the life cycle,

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:40.240
unlocked every step but you spent a lot of time talking about this particular job.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.780
Yeah. And the reason we do that is because if you can get anything right,

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:47.005
it's this- this part because it is the foundation for everything

00:08:47.005 --> 00:08:51.500
else in the product life cycle and also the easiest thing to actually screw up.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.955
The second phase, um, is- okay,

00:08:54.955 --> 00:08:56.590
once you have a problem,

00:08:56.590 --> 00:09:00.580
how do you actually decide what it is that you need to build to solve that problem.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.875
So we call this designing your solution.

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:06.490
Um, we will talk in our class but not in this webinar about,

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.980
uh, what we call the design a framework,

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:13.300
where you essentially really co- diverge in terms of brainstorming different ideas,

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.940
thinking through what the right design constraints are in order for

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.775
you to constrain the infinite ideas that you could have.

00:09:18.775 --> 00:09:21.040
And then really start to converge, right?

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.270
So you know, "Hey,

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.635
what shall I actually put aside?

00:09:23.635 --> 00:09:25.270
How do I protect this cheaply?"

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:29.320
And use my project to identify whether or not I built the most,

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:31.420
um, the cheapest MVP.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.280
So minimal- Minimum Viable Product,

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:34.675
in order to solve that problem.

00:09:34.675 --> 00:09:36.250
Yeah. And one of the things, again,

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:38.905
we spend a lot of time around the course is, um,

00:09:38.905 --> 00:09:41.320
for projects to have sufficient complexity and there's usually

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:44.800
a very large possible number of ways you could solve the problem.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:48.910
So how do you get the balance right between exploring all the ways to solve the problems,

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:51.625
which will take infinite time or will be very expensive,

00:09:51.625 --> 00:09:53.890
and making sure that you're building the best possible thing?

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:56.155
Yeah. And we also talk a lot about,

00:09:56.155 --> 00:09:59.980
how do you know that prototype that you've built is actually good enough,

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.080
passes enough of the quality bar where you

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:05.530
actually understand that it's something that people would use.

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:07.510
All right. The next phase,

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:09.850
and it's really much tied to what Anand was saying earlier

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:12.230
around shipping is a big part of what you have to do.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.065
The next phase is building, right?

00:10:14.065 --> 00:10:16.900
So once you know what solution you should build,

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:18.910
you actually need to be able to guide to your team,

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:20.200
um, in order to build it.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:21.610
And so as a product manager,

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.440
what's interesting is, um,

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:27.940
you actually don't have any direct lines of reporting- reporting typically on the team,

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:30.460
so your engineers report to an engineering manager,

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.830
you're designer re- will report to a design manager.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:38.440
How do you actually motivate them and help them get to a building a product that really,

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.990
really works and it's beautiful, right?

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:44.515
And so in- in this section we essentially talk about,

00:10:44.515 --> 00:10:48.220
uh, that iteration of taking something that's a prototype,

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.515
being able to get to the point where it is

00:10:50.515 --> 00:10:53.380
hardened enough for it to be shipped to the world,

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:55.150
and all of the phases in terms of

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:59.005
getting- getting that feedback from the different user groups.

00:10:59.005 --> 00:11:01.720
All right. The next phase,

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:04.615
um, is actually shipping it out to the world.

00:11:04.615 --> 00:11:09.100
And so the part here that's important to know is that just because you built a product,

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:12.190
it doesn't mean that everyone in the world is gonna start using it.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:16.630
And so it's- it's actually really important to understand how people start to,

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:19.840
um, learn about your product like a marketing perspective?

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:20.980
How will they start to,

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:22.840
um, use your product?

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:24.670
How will they start to pay for your product?

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:27.460
And so history is really littered with lots of examples,

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.890
where even though someone built an amazing product,

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:33.085
it doesn't mean that that led to them winning of the market.

00:11:33.085 --> 00:11:36.400
And so in our course, we talk a lot about what are the strategies that you can

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:40.990
deploy to make sure that you are shipping and marching in a way that,

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:45.775
um, is thoughtful about gaining market share. Anything to add?

00:11:45.775 --> 00:11:46.450
No.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:49.690
All right. The next phase is measuring.

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:52.180
So essentially, you have launched it to the world.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:54.760
How do you start to understand through data

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:57.670
that you're actually solving a problem that set out to solve?

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:01.135
And so this is really around, um, you know,

00:12:01.135 --> 00:12:03.010
getting the right information and where to get

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:07.040
the next phase of this- by my example, which is iteration.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:09.820
And so yeah, this is the last piece.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:13.600
How do you start to iterate and create a real feedback what we're taking the

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:17.665
both of quantitative and the qualitative data that you're learning about your product,

00:12:17.665 --> 00:12:21.940
and feed it back into an actual roadmap where you were able to say,

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:23.905
"Hey, this is where we are today.

00:12:23.905 --> 00:12:25.285
This is where we need to go.

00:12:25.285 --> 00:12:27.985
Going back to the concept of vision and the end state.

00:12:27.985 --> 00:12:30.115
Here's how we're going to keep iterating,

00:12:30.115 --> 00:12:31.990
keep measuring, um, to get there."

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:33.670
And I think what's really important to point out is,

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:36.175
what we've just laid out for you in terms of product life cycle,

00:12:36.175 --> 00:12:38.080
is this constant iterative loop,

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.275
where you're gonna be running this over and over again as you work on

00:12:41.275 --> 00:12:46.510
V2 of your product or even just a feature that's added to the product that you designed.

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:50.440
Cool. So last thing to stress,

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.705
this is not a waterfall process.

00:12:52.705 --> 00:12:55.150
Um, I think you- you probably have heard this terminology before,

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:57.415
you know, agile versus waterfall, all of that.

00:12:57.415 --> 00:13:00.190
The most important thing to remember is that if going to details

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:02.770
on these definitions is it needs to be iterative,

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:04.855
where your constantly collecting feedback,

00:13:04.855 --> 00:13:06.640
you're constantly challenging your assumptions,

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.970
and you always have in mind what is it- what is it that you're trying to test.

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:13.150
And so it is not something where it's a straight line from the beginning to

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:17.020
the end. All right.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:21.430
So again, I think to kind of like turn that into a picture,

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:25.105
it is a loop where essentially designing and testing constantly.

00:13:25.105 --> 00:13:27.625
Um, and just like an onset at the beginning,

00:13:27.625 --> 00:13:29.980
this is something that should be pretty familiar to a lot of you.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.800
I think when we were in elementary school, we learned about, you know,

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:35.980
the scientific method where you have a hypothesis and test it.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:37.375
And so really that's what this is,

00:13:37.375 --> 00:13:42.340
how do you actually create a process where you start with, um, a hypothesis,

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.145
how do you start to test in an unbiased way,

00:13:45.145 --> 00:13:47.380
how do you have the right guide for some manual process,

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:49.780
and then feed that back in to whatever it is you're

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:52.750
doing so that you're learning, um, as you're iterating.

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:56.110
Okay. So do you see,

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:58.000
we gave you an overview of, um,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.550
the product life cycle or at least our understanding of it.

00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:03.880
And we want you just today is spend a little bit more time talking about

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:08.125
the first piece of the product life cycle which is all about evaluating opportunities.

