people line up outside of the stores at

00:19:00.115 --> 00:19:02.530
midnight and camp out and wait to buy their things.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:04.690
That's what you want, right? It's very clear that you're

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:07.495
generating some real value from a customer perspective,

00:19:07.495 --> 00:19:10.675
if people are demonstrating that sort of behavior.

00:19:10.675 --> 00:19:13.555
And again, what it means to be good for the companies that you

00:19:13.555 --> 00:19:16.330
capture some meaningful value from solving the problem.

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:19.450
And the emphasis- and there kind of a lot of different ways you can do this.

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:24.610
Um, you can try and capture a little bit of value from very large number of customers,

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:26.320
or very large number of transactions,

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.585
and Amazon again, is a great example here.

00:19:28.585 --> 00:19:30.010
Um, they have very low margins,

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:31.795
but they sell so much volume,

00:19:31.795 --> 00:19:34.090
they are able to capture a very meaningful value.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.340
Um, you can also be on the other end of the spectrum.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:39.175
Er, you could be more like Oracle and more like Workday.

00:19:39.175 --> 00:19:41.290
They have a very small number of customers.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:44.770
If you capture it potentially tens of billions or hundreds of millions of dollars,

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:46.030
and that is a lot of them.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.780
There are a lots of ways to build a successful business on top of this, and in the class,

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:51.070
we spent some time talking about

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:53.620
some differences between the two models that we just talked about.

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:56.320
But fundamentally, your job as a product manager is to

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.945
make sure that you start with a customer problem,

00:19:58.945 --> 00:20:02.360
and then you believe that you captured value from them.

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:06.175
We talked a bit about the difference in a problem and an opportunity.

00:20:06.175 --> 00:20:07.870
Hopefully, you should at least have a loose sense at

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.390
this point what it means for somebody in an opportunity.

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:12.400
Now, let's talk a little bit about problem definition.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:15.940
Um, and one of the really common things we is,

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:18.175
er, you start off by talking to your customer.

00:20:18.175 --> 00:20:20.520
Uh, a product manager goes out in the field,

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.829
they spend a bunch of time talking to customers,

00:20:22.829 --> 00:20:25.845
the customer asks- asked for XYZ thing,

00:20:25.845 --> 00:20:27.435
and the product manager says, "Great."

00:20:27.435 --> 00:20:30.310
They go back to their team and they say, "We should build this."

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:34.030
Um, your fundamental job as a product manager, often is to translate,

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:38.500
I want blank for my customer and it is something that may not be that.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:40.000
The problem with this approach is,

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.850
that it's fundamentally flawed and it really can

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.790
replace two key concepts that we wanna talk about today,

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:47.530
the problem space and the solution space.

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:48.850
Um, there are many,

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:53.460
many examples from both our careers as well as from history where,

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:55.860
you go talk to a customer,

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:57.465
they tell you exactly what they want,

00:20:57.465 --> 00:20:59.805
and build that thing, and it does nothing.

00:20:59.805 --> 00:21:02.070
And fundamentally what you're doing in this world,

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:04.590
is that you're delegating product management to your customers,

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:06.540
and they're not professionals in this job.

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:09.610
Um, there's a little bit of a trope here,

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:10.990
that I wanna be careful about.

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:13.180
Um, there's an apocryphal quo- quote that

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:15.430
Henry Ford maybe said and the jury's still out.

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:17.500
Um, "If I'd asked people what they wanted,

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.435
they would have said faster horses" and of course,

00:21:19.435 --> 00:21:23.110
he's famous for producing the first mass produced car, the Model-T.

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:25.315
What we're not saying here is,

00:21:25.315 --> 00:21:26.965
don't listen to your customers.

00:21:26.965 --> 00:21:29.500
What we're saying is that, your job is to take

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:32.350
a stated solution that a customer gives you,

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:34.765
translate that back into a problem,

00:21:34.765 --> 00:21:38.080
and then re-figure out the right way to solve that problem.

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:40.735
And the framework there that we use for talking about this is called the,

00:21:40.735 --> 00:21:42.790
problem space and the solution space.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:45.925
And again, these are absolutely key concepts for product management,

00:21:45.925 --> 00:21:49.075
and really the heart of what we wanted to speak about here today.

00:21:49.075 --> 00:21:52.180
The problem space is all about where you talk about,

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:54.640
what problem you're solving for the customer,

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:57.160
and has nothing to do with how you are going to

00:21:57.160 --> 00:22:00.100
solve that problem, entirely solution agnostic.

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:01.855
The solutions space on the other hand,

00:22:01.855 --> 00:22:04.090
requires a prerequisite understanding of

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:07.465
a problem space and it's all where you talk about, how to solve that problem.

00:22:07.465 --> 00:22:10.810
And we will walk through a little bit more detail hoping to make it real.

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:14.275
Um, when a- let's take the Henry Ford example.

00:22:14.275 --> 00:22:17.950
Um, if Henry Ford had gone to a customer and said, "What do you want?"

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:19.435
And they said a faster horse,

00:22:19.435 --> 00:22:21.790
what a customer is pitching as a solution.

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:24.820
They are pitching a solution to an unstated problem,

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:28.795
which is, I wish it took less time to get from point A to point B.

00:22:28.795 --> 00:22:32.905
Your job as the product manager is to hear the words faster horse,

00:22:32.905 --> 00:22:37.750
and internalize it, actually what they were wishing for is a different transit method.

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:39.985
The problem space has no solutions allowed.

00:22:39.985 --> 00:22:41.485
We're not buying that faster anything,

00:22:41.485 --> 00:22:43.960
better anything, uh, how to do anything different,

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:49.255
your only job is to understand what your customers believe and feel today.

00:22:49.255 --> 00:22:53.335
It's very much you read all these mode of listening to your customers.

00:22:53.335 --> 00:22:56.080
A huge thing to emphasize is about,

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:59.890
understanding reality for your customers even if they can't articulate it well and,

00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:01.750
even if it's not objectively true.

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:04.570
I have definitely ran into multiple scenarios in my career

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:07.915
where a customer believes something that is not correct.

00:23:07.915 --> 00:23:10.120
Um, they believe a product doesn't have a certain feature,

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:11.575
or that it can't do something else.

00:23:11.575 --> 00:23:15.355
It doesn't matter. This is not the point to pitch them on it, or sell them on it.

00:23:15.355 --> 00:23:17.740
Your job here is to listen and understand

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:20.305
what they believe and understand what they feel,

00:23:20.305 --> 00:23:23.065
and then figure out later how to solve it.

00:23:23.065 --> 00:23:25.630
The solution space is where you start to

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:28.030
introduce the notion of how we are going to solve the problem.

00:23:28.030 --> 00:23:30.895
And again, the model that we keep coming back to is,

00:23:30.895 --> 00:23:32.950
you must talk to your customers in your beginning.

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:36.325
Your customers will model these two concepts, your job is to,

00:23:36.325 --> 00:23:40.900
is to distill out of those conversations the exact problem and then,

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:42.460
retranslate it to a solution.

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:45.490
Er, so another way of thinking about this is,

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:47.845
the problem space is entirely defined by our customer.

00:23:47.845 --> 00:23:49.330
You have zero input here,

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:51.085
and zero right to comment on it.

00:23:51.085 --> 00:23:53.290
But the solution space should be defined by you.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:55.615
And if you draw the clean distinction between them,

00:23:55.615 --> 00:23:58.120
it means that your customers are not doing your product management,

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:01.795
and you're not, er, sort of crossing the net and feeling alive on your customer.

00:24:01.795 --> 00:24:04.720
We know a lot of this can sound fairly abstract,

