you pause there, you actually go and talk to your users.

00:24:42.815 --> 00:24:44.980
So in this example,

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:46.195
you say to yourself, "Okay,

00:24:46.195 --> 00:24:49.930
I'm going to find what my target customer actually is." This is really important.

00:24:49.930 --> 00:24:51.520
You need to actually to go out and say,

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:53.290
"I am going to focus on these people,

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.075
and not these people."

00:24:55.075 --> 00:24:56.410
So you're, you're gonna go out and say,

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:58.030
"I'm gonna focus on people who bike,

00:24:58.030 --> 00:25:01.210
but I'm not gonna focus on the people who take public transport,

00:25:01.210 --> 00:25:05.395
for example, you know, take the subway or take the BART or the Muni, or whatever that is."

00:25:05.395 --> 00:25:07.690
So this is important because,

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:13.645
if you define your target user and anyone who might- going back to the previous slide,

00:25:13.645 --> 00:25:15.955
be part of the- the,

00:25:15.955 --> 00:25:18.145
you know, solution in reducing the carbon footprint,

00:25:18.145 --> 00:25:19.945
you could be talking to a lot of people,

00:25:19.945 --> 00:25:22.975
and you need to be able to prioritize your time,

00:25:22.975 --> 00:25:25.120
and- and who you're designing for.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:27.390
So, okay. Let's stay on this case, your are like, "All right,

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:30.400
my current customer is just- just the people who bike.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:34.240
Let me go talk to them and let me hear what it is that they are struggling with."

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:38.920
And so you go out there, and you hear a bunch of statements that sound a lot like,

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:40.015
what Anand was just saying,

00:25:40.015 --> 00:25:43.900
where solutions that the customers think they want something,

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:46.370
and they're telling you what they think they want feels.

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:49.015
So you go there- you go out there and you basically hear,

00:25:49.015 --> 00:25:51.280
one, "I wish I had an electric bike.

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:54.700
Hills are terrible and electric bikes would really help me."

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:56.860
The second thing hear is, "You know,

00:25:56.860 --> 00:26:00.340
I really wish I had a better helmet because it, it- they're so expensive".

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:03.310
Um, you know, "If had a better helmet, cheaper,

00:26:03.310 --> 00:26:05.020
I would just feel much more comfortable biking

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:07.180
and therefore I can help you to reduce the carbon footprint."

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:09.865
The last one is, "Okay, I don't bike a lot.

00:26:09.865 --> 00:26:11.800
You know, I really wish we could fix the roads.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:14.485
The road quality is really like terrible.

00:26:14.485 --> 00:26:16.165
So you know, if you did that,

00:26:16.165 --> 00:26:17.785
then I will be comfortable biking."

00:26:17.785 --> 00:26:22.515
So let's say these are the three buckets of things that you're hearing from your user,

00:26:22.515 --> 00:26:24.135
when you go out there to ask them,

00:26:24.135 --> 00:26:26.940
why do you not bike as much as you could be biking?

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:30.105
Or like, what could we do to help you bike more?

00:26:30.105 --> 00:26:31.845
So it's pretty easy.

00:26:31.845 --> 00:26:34.935
Well, a lot of times your product managers can hear this

00:26:34.935 --> 00:26:38.490
and they think that translates into a solution thing they feel.

00:26:38.490 --> 00:26:39.870
So in the first case, you are like,

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:41.970
oh, someone wants an electric bike.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.935
If only I could give them a cheap,

00:26:43.935 --> 00:26:48.565
usable electric bike, maybe they will actually bike where there are hills.

00:26:48.565 --> 00:26:50.950
Um, you might, in the case of, you know,

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:52.615
the next line, for the better helmet,

00:26:52.615 --> 00:26:54.190
you're like, "I just need to make,

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:56.200
uh, a, you know, you know, safer one,

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:57.385
a less expensive one,

00:26:57.385 --> 00:27:00.235
and I give that to them, they will start biking more.

00:27:00.235 --> 00:27:03.970
But this is kind of the big fallacy that we were just talking about before,

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:06.745
if you just listen to what they said they wanted,

00:27:06.745 --> 00:27:08.530
and then you built it for them,

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:12.010
it doesn't necessarily mean that you're solving the crux their problem,

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:15.295
or why they're truly not biking in the first place.

00:27:15.295 --> 00:27:19.630
So let's assume that you're in a situation and you dig a level deeper.

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:23.035
So instead of just hearing what they said and doing it,

00:27:23.035 --> 00:27:24.760
you start to try to understand,

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:28.960
why is it that they are claiming that they want a certain situation?

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:33.385
And instead, you find out something pretty different than your first reaction.

00:27:33.385 --> 00:27:35.440
So in the case of the person who is saying,

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:37.315
"Well, I really wish I had an electric bike."

00:27:37.315 --> 00:27:40.360
What they are really upset about and what caused them to not

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:46.345
want to bike is that, because there are hills, by the time they bike into work,

00:27:46.345 --> 00:27:48.685
they are essentially super sweaty and they, you know,

00:27:48.685 --> 00:27:51.670
don't have a way to essentially go from that mode

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:55.195
into [LAUGHTER] into like a clean mode where they can go in to work.

00:27:55.195 --> 00:28:00.280
And so you actually learn from that user that they like biking,

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:02.350
um, they get exercise from biking.

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:06.010
But because they get so sweaty from all of that biking,

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:07.945
they are reluctant to actually bike.

00:28:07.945 --> 00:28:10.900
So that's a fundamentally different problem than,

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:12.985
"Hey, give me an electric bike?"

00:28:12.985 --> 00:28:16.390
In fact, if you build an electric bike for that person,

00:28:16.390 --> 00:28:18.835
you might actually find that there- they

00:28:18.835 --> 00:28:21.880
crave the exercise they used to be getting when they were biking.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:24.520
Um, so that's- that's why it's so important to actually

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:27.580
understand why they're asking for the things they are asking for.

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:29.065
And the second case, um,

00:28:29.065 --> 00:28:31.300
you actually learn that the reason [NOISE] why someone wants

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:34.885
a better helmet is because they're super afraid of getting hit by a car.

00:28:34.885 --> 00:28:39.235
So in their mind one of the best ways to kind of guarantee that is, you know,

00:28:39.235 --> 00:28:41.170
protection and protection to them

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:44.830
translates- translates into helmets as opposed to anything else.

00:28:44.830 --> 00:28:47.020
And finally, just to go through these examples,

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:50.500
in the last case that a person who might be really worried about road quality

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:54.205
at first is actually truly worried about falling.

00:28:54.205 --> 00:28:57.220
They're worried that with the roads the way they are,

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:59.800
they're gonna go through the bumps and end up falling.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:03.535
So [NOISE] if you

00:29:03.535 --> 00:29:07.825
actually understand the second level of what it is that they are struggling with,

00:29:07.825 --> 00:29:09.895
you might have a very different solution.

00:29:09.895 --> 00:29:15.430
So the solution that you would land on instead in the case of, "Hey, you know,

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:17.140
I really feel like I want to

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:20.260
exercise," but you realize that what they're actually saying is,

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:23.755
"I want a way to not be super sweaty when I get to work."

00:29:23.755 --> 00:29:27.280
Your solution there could actually be building showers at work.

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:30.535
Fundamentally, different solution than giving them electric bikes.

00:29:30.535 --> 00:29:33.520
In the second user's scenario, um,

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.350
where they're asking for a better helmet but

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:38.425
their real problem is that they're afraid of getting hit by a car,

00:29:38.425 --> 00:29:40.810
you know, a helmet is a reactive thing,

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:44.515
it's like after you get hit by a car how do we make sure that you are,

00:29:44.515 --> 00:29:47.005
you know, not as hurt as you could have been.

00:29:47.005 --> 00:29:49.255
Instead the right solution could be to build

00:29:49.255 --> 00:29:54.025
protective bike lanes where cars can't go into those bike lanes and hit the biker.

00:29:54.025 --> 00:29:57.580
And then finally, in the case of the person who's really worried of falling,

00:29:57.580 --> 00:29:59.515
instead of just fixing the roads for them,

00:29:59.515 --> 00:30:02.635
a much cheaper thing to do would be providing them with a,

00:30:02.635 --> 00:30:04.690
um, providing them with a bike with better suspension.

00:30:04.690 --> 00:30:08.925
You know, think about how much that cost versus having to repave all your roads,

00:30:08.925 --> 00:30:11.340
that solution is gonna be much more

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:15.370
sustainable and directly solve the problem that that user is facing.

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:19.120
We wanted to walk through these examples to just try and make this a little bit real,

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:21.880
we know the- the fire slides could be abstract.

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:25.615
So hopefully that gives you some idea of what we wanted to talk about today.

00:30:25.615 --> 00:30:29.305
And again, we cover a lot of this in the class and I think Pax mention in a couple minutes.

00:30:29.305 --> 00:30:31.750
Really our goal is to get to a place where

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:35.620
these people who take the course feel like they're capable and qualified

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:38.830
to either be better product managers if they are already product managers

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:42.520
today or confident that they have the knowledge they need to break into the field.

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:45.550
So with that I think we'll turn it back over to Pax,

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:47.980
um, Pax wanna take it away.

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:51.865
So with that, I want to move into some Q&amp;A.

00:30:51.865 --> 00:30:55.570
Um, we have been getting really a lot of excellent questions,

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:58.120
Anand and JZ and kind of looking through them

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:00.580
there are three basic, uh, categories.

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:03.250
So I think the first one building off

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:06.460
of what we were just talking about in terms of the course there are questions around,

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:08.620
how do you become a product manager?

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:10.810
Um, and so maybe just starting there,

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:14.770
what would you- if you're say for instance a- a enterprise solution business analyst,

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:17.170
we have a very specific example and you are interested in

00:31:17.170 --> 00:31:19.900
making that transition into being a product manager,

00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:22.840
uh, what advice would you have?

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:28.210
Um, my experience is that the unfortunate reality

00:31:28.210 --> 00:31:29.650
is that the best predictor of getting

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:32.740
a product management job is already having a product management job.

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:36.430
Um, having said that though there are like three or call it

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:42.050
common tasks that probably make up 75 or 80 percent of the people that I see.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:46.810
Um, one of them tends to be more of an option for I say junior people,

00:31:46.810 --> 00:31:50.425
um, which is like a structured training and development program.

00:31:50.425 --> 00:31:52.780
Um, Google is famous for its APM program,

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:54.430
Yahoo has one, Microsoft has one,

00:31:54.430 --> 00:31:55.750
Facebook has one, Dropbox has one.

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:57.910
So there's a whole set of companies that actually have

00:31:57.910 --> 00:31:59.890
a structured program that

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:02.950
will take people who do not have a product management background,

00:32:02.950 --> 00:32:06.325
teach them the skills usually in the order of about two years.

00:32:06.325 --> 00:32:08.080
Um, and then they're either rotated throughout

00:32:08.080 --> 00:32:09.910
the company or they get a job somewhere else.

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:15.115
Um, that is a- usually a good career options for people who are early in their careers

00:32:15.115 --> 00:32:17.860
or people who are transitioning like another way I see that

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:20.500
happen a lot is somebody ends up at business school,

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:22.630
then they end up doing a rotational program.

00:32:22.630 --> 00:32:25.045
Um, but if you are later in your career,

00:32:25.045 --> 00:32:27.310
it obviously has some costs associated with it,

00:32:27.310 --> 00:32:28.500
it may not be something you wouldn't wanna do.

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:30.230
That's option one that I've seen a lot.

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:32.680
Option two that I've seen is have

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:38.500
some fundamental knowledge about your customers that is being used for the

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:42.880
product management and the two really common teams that I see transition the most

00:32:42.880 --> 00:32:47.980
often from an internal transfer perspective are something around like customer success,

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:50.425
customer support, and something around sales.

00:32:50.425 --> 00:32:53.860
And the reason that both of those are true is because both of those jobs

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:57.790
fundamentally require you to have a very strong understanding of your customers.

00:32:57.790 --> 00:32:59.710
What those people can often bring especially in

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:02.050
larger organizations is less of the knowledge about

00:33:02.050 --> 00:33:03.730
product management and discipline but

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:06.535
a very high level of knowledge about your customers.

00:33:06.535 --> 00:33:08.545
And you sort of have this interesting pair where

00:33:08.545 --> 00:33:10.705
they'll often get paired with a peer mentor,

00:33:10.705 --> 00:33:13.435
that mentor teaches that person product management skills

00:33:13.435 --> 00:33:16.255
that person makes sure that they have to call a customer.

00:33:16.255 --> 00:33:18.580
So if you're interested in that route,

00:33:18.580 --> 00:33:20.800
obviously it depends on your company and a bunch of other things.

00:33:20.800 --> 00:33:22.810
Um, but making sure that you represent

00:33:22.810 --> 00:33:26.290
a very strong voice of the customer and find for them what is important.

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:31.285
Um the last one is a- it's a more of a Silicon Valley option.

00:33:31.285 --> 00:33:33.925
So I recognize this is not necessarily ideal for everyone.

00:33:33.925 --> 00:33:36.910
So a lot of people who end up as product managers actually

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:41.275
started companies that either were successful or were not successful.

00:33:41.275 --> 00:33:45.115
Um, it's pretty common in my experience at least in the places that we've worked where

00:33:45.115 --> 00:33:49.090
founders of companies end up in product management whether it's through acquisitions,

00:33:49.090 --> 00:33:51.220
whether it's through a failed company or the next job.

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:55.450
Um, I'm definitely not advocating that you start a failed company rather than a PM job,

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:57.520
that's not the most efficient path to get in there.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:34:00.430
But those 3 categories probably make

00:34:00.430 --> 00:34:03.940
up 80 percent of the people that I know in product management.

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:05.215
Yeah, and just two things to add.

00:34:05.215 --> 00:34:06.310
So I know that, you know,

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:07.630
you've mentioned you are business,

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:10.180
um, you know, analyst, a solution analyst.

00:34:10.180 --> 00:34:12.610
I think that having- in that role,

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:15.370
you actually gain a lot of knowledge around the actual product,

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:17.890
like you are actually going in there and you're saying, "Hey,

00:34:17.890 --> 00:34:22.030
in order for us to deploy or this innovation to work like these things need to happen."

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:24.070
And so that kinda fits into the second one,

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:25.570
um, that Anand was describing.

00:34:25.570 --> 00:34:28.570
And I'd say that the last one it's not just about founding a company,

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:30.370
and you can also join a startup, right?

00:34:30.370 --> 00:34:32.185
And at a startup, it's- there's always

00:34:32.185 --> 00:34:35.320
too many jobs for the number of people who are there,

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:37.555
and so you get to wear a lot of different hats.

00:34:37.555 --> 00:34:40.300
And in that case, you can wear the hat of being an engineer but

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:43.120
at the same time wear the product hat because you're essentially, you know,

00:34:43.120 --> 00:34:46.525
in a company where there aren't product managers and you as an engineer needs to decide,

00:34:46.525 --> 00:34:49.375
you know, what is the problem and how do you actually implement it?

00:34:49.375 --> 00:34:52.120
And so that's another option which is not quite the be

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:56.050
a founder path but go to a startup and wear a lot of different hats there.

00:34:56.050 --> 00:34:57.130
And then fun fact,

00:34:57.130 --> 00:34:58.300
how did- how did you,

00:34:58.300 --> 00:35:00.010
uh, solve transition too?

00:35:00.010 --> 00:35:04.720
Sure, uh, the way I actually ended up in product management was I

00:35:04.720 --> 00:35:07.060
worked at a startup as an engineer

00:35:07.060 --> 00:35:09.655
and doing like chemical engineering and process engineering,

00:35:09.655 --> 00:35:13.900
and I got to pick my title and I just picked product manager arbitrarily.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:15.880
[NOISE] And when I interviewed at my next role,

00:35:15.880 --> 00:35:19.645
they said we see you have product management experience. And I said yes.

00:35:19.645 --> 00:35:23.260
Um, so I'm probably the- the living example of how not to do it.

00:35:23.260 --> 00:35:26.110
Um, but that was a total case where the best predictor

00:35:26.110 --> 00:35:29.840
of a product management job was already having a product management job.

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:34.450
That was great, um, I wanna build on something you said a Anand.

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:37.045
So you- you said that you see a lot of people transit-

00:35:37.045 --> 00:35:39.850
transitioning from customer success and sales positions.

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:42.505
I think there's sometimes a perception that you need to have

00:35:42.505 --> 00:35:46.105
a strong technical background to be a successful product manager.

00:35:46.105 --> 00:35:47.830
Do you have any reflections on that?

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:50.785
What level of technical expertise is that going to depend on

00:35:50.785 --> 00:35:54.400
the- the product or the company or- [OVERLAPPING] Ye yeah.

00:35:54.400 --> 00:35:56.680
It is going to depend on a bunch of things for sure.

00:35:56.680 --> 00:35:58.180
Um, like if, uh,

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:01.390
the probability of you transitioning to be an infrastructure PM,

00:36:01.390 --> 00:36:05.200
um, is probably not super high if you were in customer support right now.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:06.640
So it's a little bit role dependent.

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:08.860
But the way I thought about this previously and

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:11.710
actually for people that I've hired is what's

00:36:11.710 --> 00:36:17.455
not actually important is some notion of a degree or explicit credentials in that thing.

00:36:17.455 --> 00:36:20.350
You don't need need a CS degree, you don't need, um,

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:23.650
what's much more important is that you

00:36:23.650 --> 00:36:26.305
have a detailed understanding of how the product works.

00:36:26.305 --> 00:36:27.655
And if it's a software product,

00:36:27.655 --> 00:36:30.385
kind of something fundamental about how computers work.

00:36:30.385 --> 00:36:32.290
There are many, many ways to get that.

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:34.450
Some of the best PMs I know actually did not have

00:36:34.450 --> 00:36:37.540
formal computer science training but spend a lot of the time when

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:39.760
they were 17 to 22 like

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:43.015
building stuff and taking things apart and putting them back together,

00:36:43.015 --> 00:36:45.175
in many, many ways to get that knowledge.

00:36:45.175 --> 00:36:46.810
And one of the things I see people

00:36:46.810 --> 00:36:50.035
overemphasize is like the signaling value of that knowledge,

00:36:50.035 --> 00:36:52.660
whether it's trying to get experience or something else,

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:55.495
in my experience it has been much more important to be

00:36:55.495 --> 00:36:59.065
credible for the team that is responsible for building the thing.

00:36:59.065 --> 00:37:01.630
And that is really the bar. There are many ways to accomplish

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:05.455
that whether cheap or less time than are required.

00:37:05.455 --> 00:37:08.130
One other thing to add there is,

00:37:08.130 --> 00:37:10.410
um, there are different types of PM roles, right?

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:12.480
So you can be a role PM,

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:14.445
you can be more of a generalist PM,

00:37:14.445 --> 00:37:16.200
