00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:28.090
If you have been a product manager

00:45:28.090 --> 00:45:30.790
for a year and a half chances are you're thinking about

00:45:30.790 --> 00:45:33.910
very large macro things are pretty low usually you are

00:45:33.910 --> 00:45:37.210
focused on the next thing next week next month.

00:45:37.210 --> 00:45:40.660
And then you get more senior you actually end up more unto the ladder.

00:45:40.660 --> 00:45:45.940
The way that we strike a balance between them is a big part of my job as the VP of

00:45:45.940 --> 00:45:48.250
product management a pilot is make sure we have

00:45:48.250 --> 00:45:51.160
the right balance of short-term versus long-term staff.

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:53.500
There's very much like a-a failure mode of

00:45:53.500 --> 00:45:55.540
product management organizations where you

00:45:55.540 --> 00:45:58.570
believe that the next feature is a magical thing right?

00:45:58.570 --> 00:46:01.120
This is the thing that works whatever we're

00:46:01.120 --> 00:46:03.790
trying to accomplish and more often than not it's not true.

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:08.260
So we actually have in our roadmap setting process a process that

00:46:08.260 --> 00:46:12.760
I run where you sort of classify everything into small local optimizers and

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:14.820
global optimizers. The local optimizers are like

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:18.220
Here's the set of- here's the product we have today.

00:46:18.220 --> 00:46:20.890
We can do a bunch of stuff only 10 at percent better, 20 percent better,

00:46:20.890 --> 00:46:23.830
50 percent better, but cumulatively in five years,

00:46:23.830 --> 00:46:25.600
we want to be a 1,000 percent better.

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:27.580
We need to make some bigger bets,

00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:31.525
on things that we believe will fundamentally change our products like 2_x or by 5_x.

00:46:31.525 --> 00:46:34.435
They tend to be more expensive and they tend to be much riskier.

00:46:34.435 --> 00:46:37.720
But I'm really looking for is like a risk-blended portfolio,

00:46:37.720 --> 00:46:39.340
where I know that we have a set of

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:42.400
small things that I know we're going to do deliver short-term result,

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:45.520
instead of bigger things that actually may or may not deliver long-term outcomes.

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:48.309
But my job is to be the key of the organization,

00:46:48.309 --> 00:46:49.780
to make sure we're both thinking the same.

00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:52.600
And do you think that,

00:46:52.600 --> 00:46:54.850
at all times, your company,

00:46:54.850 --> 00:46:56.980
your team, your organization, whatever it is,

00:46:56.980 --> 00:47:00.640
should have a long-term view on what it- what it is it's trying to accomplish?

00:47:00.640 --> 00:47:03.010
And a lot of the PM role is actually translating, uh,

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:05.500
wait, connecting the dots between all the different levels.

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:07.330
So we talked about company mobile vision.

