00:50:25.765 --> 00:50:28.900
Like, and it's the question I asked a lot in product use, product review.

00:50:28.900 --> 00:50:31.300
If you have some idea for something you wanna try and

00:50:31.300 --> 00:50:34.000
the cost of being wrong is like a day of engineering, right?

00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:35.860
Great, no need to wait anymore, go for it.

00:50:35.860 --> 00:50:38.410
Seems fine, right? But if- again, let's talk,

00:50:38.410 --> 00:50:41.965
let's talk about the space shuttle or like an airplane or a bridge, right.

00:50:41.965 --> 00:50:45.580
If the cost of being wrong is very very large whether it's human life,

00:50:45.580 --> 00:50:48.625
whether it's large dollar cost or about something else, then you better be right.

00:50:48.625 --> 00:50:50.755
So I think a lot about the, the,

00:50:50.755 --> 00:50:55.030
cost of being wrong, and like the downside of failure relative to the investment.

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:58.465
And then I really want you both to notice when the organization is confused,

00:50:58.465 --> 00:51:00.580
and then we push towards decisiveness.

00:51:00.580 --> 00:51:03.340
Yeah. One principle that's related to that that's easy for people to

00:51:03.340 --> 00:51:05.950
visualize is a concept of a one-way door, right.

00:51:05.950 --> 00:51:07.330
So like, you know,

00:51:07.330 --> 00:51:08.710
if you are actually going through a door,

00:51:08.710 --> 00:51:10.465
what's really hard to kind of back out off,

00:51:10.465 --> 00:51:13.360
be very thoughtful about that decision but if you're like, look,

00:51:13.360 --> 00:51:17.720
I can keep trying it and work that quickly, that's totally fine.

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:22.030
That's fantastic. We're, we're nearing the end of our time here.

00:51:22.030 --> 00:51:25.105
So I think maybe we have time for one more quick question,

00:51:25.105 --> 00:51:28.705
um, and then we'll, we'll wrap up.

00:51:28.705 --> 00:51:32.530
So another question that came up several times is,

00:51:32.530 --> 00:51:35.440
a lot of product teams can have a hard time saying,

00:51:35.440 --> 00:51:37.690
no, saying no to engineering, saying no to the CEO.

00:51:37.690 --> 00:51:41.070
So how do you- especially coming back to your earlier point that you don't

00:51:41.070 --> 00:51:45.120
always have decision-making authority within the context of the larger company,

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:47.770
how do you say no?

00:51:48.650 --> 00:51:51.285
I mean, negotiate that.

00:51:51.285 --> 00:51:55.900
Yeah, you know, it is your job as PM to say no,

00:51:55.900 --> 00:51:59.650
but I think the point of this question is how do you say no gracefully?

00:51:59.650 --> 00:52:04.300
And a tip that I usually give my teams is this concept of, yes and.

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:06.580
Otherwise known as provide options.

00:52:06.580 --> 00:52:08.860
And what that really means is, hey, look.

00:52:08.860 --> 00:52:11.170
You know, I understand the spirit

00:52:11.170 --> 00:52:13.270
of what you're asking for. That's actually really important.

00:52:13.270 --> 00:52:15.415
A lot of times people going back to, you know,

00:52:15.415 --> 00:52:17.950
users being confused, CEOs can be confused,

00:52:17.950 --> 00:52:19.480
people in the company can be confused.

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:21.355
They can be like, I want this to happen,

00:52:21.355 --> 00:52:24.460
but really what they're hoping for is something else.

00:52:24.460 --> 00:52:28.660
Right. Or like I understand the spirit of what it is that needs to happen.

00:52:28.660 --> 00:52:32.140
Translate that into a problem statement and then say,

00:52:32.140 --> 00:52:35.500
here are the options that we could go through and provide.

00:52:35.500 --> 00:52:36.850
We can do A, B,

00:52:36.850 --> 00:52:40.090
and C. Here is the pros and cons of each one and I,

00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:41.965
as the subject matter expert,

00:52:41.965 --> 00:52:45.805
now in your area, really recommend option B for these reasons.

00:52:45.805 --> 00:52:48.895
And so being able to provide that kind of, like, options space,

00:52:48.895 --> 00:52:50.950
and really have it be backed up by data,

00:52:50.950 --> 00:52:53.335
is really really helpful when having this conversation.

00:52:53.335 --> 00:52:55.960
The other thing that works very well for me is not to say,

00:52:55.960 --> 00:52:57.385
no, but to say, later.

00:52:57.385 --> 00:53:01.330
And what's fascinating is like how many organizational problems sort themselves out,

00:53:01.330 --> 00:53:03.850
waiting to say, later, and it never comes back.

00:53:03.850 --> 00:53:06.370
Um, the pattern that I think we

00:53:06.370 --> 00:53:09.040
actually end up building are things that, that keep coming back.

00:53:09.040 --> 00:53:12.670
Right. Like, if we say later and then a month ending here, and then a month later,

00:53:12.670 --> 00:53:14.740
and then like, it kind of a keeps coming out, then,

00:53:14.740 --> 00:53:16.930
yeah, probably a good change we're going to work on it.

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:20.350
But you'd be surprised how many things just go away if you, yes.

00:53:20.350 --> 00:53:22.855
But later, I mean, will never occur.

00:53:22.855 --> 00:53:26.950
Okay. Well, ah, thank you very much.

00:53:26.950 --> 00:53:29.650
Ah, until later but hopefully in the classroom.

00:53:29.650 --> 00:53:32.830
Ah, so we wanna thank you both for your time.

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:34.990
That was really a, a fantastic presentation.

00:53:34.990 --> 00:53:39.430
I wanna thank everyone who attended and for your questions and your feedback,

00:53:39.430 --> 00:53:40.600
we really appreciate it.

00:53:40.600 --> 00:53:43.195
Again, if you're interested more from Anand and JZ,

00:53:43.195 --> 00:53:45.385
there's an opportunity to take a class with them,

00:53:45.385 --> 00:53:46.780
uh, this winter quarter.

00:53:46.780 --> 00:53:48.490
I encourage you to check it out.

00:53:48.490 --> 00:53:51.490
And if not, hopefully we'll see you again in some other form,

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:55.225
either on a webinar or in a self-paced short-course offering.

00:53:55.225 --> 00:53:56.425
So [OVERLAPPING] thank you again.

00:53:56.425 --> 00:53:57.745
Thank you Anand and JZ.

